    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s006-1-3-1-establishing-trust",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "1.3.1. Establishing Trust",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s006-1-3-1-establishing-trust.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 1.3.1. Establishing Trust\n\nThe basis of making trust decisions in C2PA, our [trust model](#_trust_model), is the identity of the signer associated with the cryptographic signing key used to sign a claim in a C2PA Manifest. The claim signatures of C2PA Manifests, when combined with trusted time stamps, can undergo the validation process indefinitely to determine if claims were signed while the signing credentials were valid and not revoked.\n\n<a id=\"_an_example\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Trust in C2PA rests on the identity of the signer associated with the key used to sign a claim, per the specification's trust model. Combined with trusted time stamps, claim signatures can be validated indefinitely to determine whether a claim was signed while the signing credentials were valid and not revoked.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s007-1-3-2-an-example",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "1.3.2. An Example",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s007-1-3-2-an-example.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 1.3.2. An Example\n\nA very common scenario will be a user taking a photograph with their C2PA-enabled camera (or phone). In that instance, the camera would create a manifest containing some assertions including information about the camera itself, a thumbnail of the image and some cryptographic hashes that bind the photograph to the manifest. These assertions would then be listed in the Claim, which would be digitally signed and then the entire C2PA Manifest (see [Figure 2, “Example C2PA Manifest of a Photograph”](#Photo_Manifest)) would be embedded into the output JPEG. This C2PA Manifest",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A worked example: a C2PA-enabled camera or phone takes a photograph and creates a manifest whose assertions include details about the camera, a thumbnail and cryptographic hashes binding the photograph to the manifest. The assertions are listed in a claim, which is digitally signed, and the whole C2PA Manifest (Figure 2) is embedded in the output JPEG, where it remains valid indefinitely. A manifest consumer such as a validator then validates the signature and its credential, checks each assertion, and presents the information and signature so the user can make an informed decision about the content's trustworthiness.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-31"
      }
