      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "1.5. Claim Generators",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s010-1-5-claim-generators.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### 1.5. Claim Generators\n\nThe core implementation flow for Claim Generators is to create assertions, reference them from a claim, include relevant ingredients, bind the claim to the asset, sign the claim, and package everything into a valid C2PA Manifest.\n\nExamples of Claim Generators in the C2PA ecosystem include cameras, recorders, AI asset creators, asset editing software, and transcoders.\n\nMust Read to Implement a Claim Generator\n\n**[Technical Overview](#_technical_overview)**  \n**Read:** All of this section.  \n**Why:** Provides a basic mental model of how a C2PA Manifest is structured an",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A reading path for claim generators, whose core flow is to create assertions, reference them from a claim, include relevant ingredients, bind the claim to the asset, sign it, and package everything as a valid C2PA Manifest; examples are cameras, recorders, AI asset creators, editing software and transcoders. Required reading, each with its reason: the Technical Overview; selected Glossary terms; Assertions; Unique Identifiers (C2PA URNs); Binding to Content (hard bindings, and soft bindings such as watermarks for recovering a stripped manifest); Claims; URI References; Manifests; Embedding C2PA Manifests (only the supported formats); the standard assertions the generator emits; Cryptography; and parts of the Trust Model. Live Media is required only for live content, and Validation only for generators that support ingredients, since ingredients must be validated on ingestion — though any generator can use it as a QA checklist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
