      "target": "../sections/s012-1-7-security-reviewers.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### 1.7. Security Reviewers\n\nSecurity reviewers inspect the various specifications and guidance documents from the C2PA to determine whether the trust model, cryptographic algorithms, validation process, and security guidance meet current best practices.\n\nMust Read to Review C2PA Security\n\n**[Design Goals](#design-goals)**  \n**Read:** All of this section.  \n**Why:** Explains the security and provenance goals the C2PA specification is designed to support, including what the specification is intended to prove and what it is not intended to prove.\n\n**[Technical Overview](#_technical_overview)**",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A reading path for security reviewers, who check whether the trust model, cryptographic algorithms, validation process and security guidance meet current best practice. Required reading: the Design Goals (including what the specification is and is not meant to prove), the Technical Overview, Information Security Considerations (the core section, on threats, harms, misuse and abuse), the Trust Model, Cryptography and Validation. Helpful context: Assertions, Binding to Content (including the risks when assets are modified, stripped or matched by soft binding), Claims, Manifests (integrity, ambiguity, stripping and substitution risks), the relevant embedding formats, and the standard assertions relevant to the reviewer's threat model. User Experience is needed when reviewing user-facing trust signals, since misleading presentation can undermine security claims, and Live Media when reviewing live workflows.",
