      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "10.3.2.5.1. Use of RFC 3161",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s105-10-3-2-5-1-use-of-rfc-3161.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 10.3.2.5.1. Use of RFC 3161\n\nIf possible, the claim generator should use a RFC 3161-compliant Time Stamping Authority (TSA) ([RFC 3161](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3161)) to obtain a trusted time stamp proving that the signature itself actually existed at a certain date and time and incorporate that into the `COSE_Sign1_Tagged` structure as a countersignature.\n\nClaim generators are encouraged to obtain and include time stamps to ensure their manifests will remain valid. As described in [Chapter 14, _Validation_](#validation-clause), manifests without time stamps cease to be valid",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends that, if possible, the claim generator use an RFC 3161-compliant Time Stamping Authority to obtain a trusted time stamp proving the signature existed at a certain date and time, incorporated into the COSE_Sign1_Tagged structure as a countersignature. Encourages including time stamps because, per Chapter 14 Validation, manifests without them cease to be valid when the signing credential expires or is revoked. Requires a manifest to contain only one time stamp, noting that earlier versions allowed multiple.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
