      "title": "11.1. Overview",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s114-11-1-overview.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### 11.1. Overview\n\nWithin C2PA, individual statements about an asset are declared as [assertions](#_assertions). A C2PA Manifest is a container that bundles together an [assertion store](#assertion-store-arch) holding those assertions, a [claim](#_claims) that hashes the assertions and provides information about the [claim generator](#claim-generator-definition), and a [claim signature](#_digital_signatures) over that claim. The C2PA Manifest that describes the asset is called the [active manifest](#_active_manifest). Because assets can be built from [ingredients](#_adding_ingredients) that h",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes a C2PA Manifest as a container bundling an assertion store, a claim that hashes the assertions and provides information about the claim generator, and a claim signature over that claim. The manifest that describes the asset is the active manifest, and because ingredients may have their own manifests, all manifests associated with an asset are collected into a single C2PA Manifest Store. A standard C2PA Manifest includes a hard binding cryptographically associating it with the asset, so copying or linking it to a different asset is detectable and fails validation; serialization details are in Section 11.5 Serialization.",
        "state": "draft",
