    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s155-12-3-digital-signatures",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3. Digital Signatures",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s155-12-3-digital-signatures.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### 12.3. Digital Signatures\n\nAll claim signatures shall be generated using one of the digital signature algorithms and key types listed as described in this section. This section defines both:\n\n*   A list of digital signature algorithms and key types that generators should use for new claim signatures and that validators shall support for validating existing claim signatures (the allowed list);\n    \n*   A list of digital signature algorithms and key types that validators should support for validating existing claim signatures but that generators shall not use for creating new claim signatures",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Requires all claim signatures to use digital signature algorithms and key types from an allowed list (generators should use, validators shall support) or a deprecated list (validators should support, generators shall not use), excluding custom assertions and time stamps. Specifies that the lists reference COSE algorithm identifiers from standards such as RFC 9052, RFC 9053, RFC 8230 and RFC 9964, which also specify the hash used in each signature scheme. Requires that Section 12.1, Hashing, not apply to those hash algorithms, whose use is allowed and followed for any listed signature algorithm.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s156-12-3-1-signature-algorithms",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3.1. Signature Algorithms",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s156-12-3-1-signature-algorithms.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 12.3.1. Signature Algorithms\n\nThe allowed list is:\n\n*   ES256 (ECDSA with SHA-256);\n    \n*   ES384 (ECDSA with SHA-384);\n    \n*   ES512 (ECDSA with SHA-512);\n    \n*   ESP256 (ECDSA with SHA-256, fully-specified equivalent of `ES256`);\n    \n*   ESP384 (ECDSA with SHA-384, fully-specified equivalent of `ES384`);\n    \n*   ESP512 (ECDSA with SHA-512, fully-specified equivalent of `ES512`);\n    \n*   PS256 (RSASSA-PSS using SHA-256 and MGF1 with SHA-256);\n    \n*   PS384 (RSASSA-PSS using SHA-384 and MGF1 with SHA-384);\n    \n*   PS512 (RSASSA-PSS using SHA-512 and MGF1 with SHA-512);\n    \n*   Ed",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the allowed claim signature algorithms: ES256, ES384, ES512, ESP256, ESP384, ESP512, PS256, PS384, PS512, EdDSA (Ed25519 only), and ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 (COSE identifiers -48, -49, -50); the deprecated list is empty. Notes cover ML-DSA's larger signature and key sizes, the fully-specified ESP identifiers binding each hash to one curve, and that certificate signing algorithms are governed separately by Section 13.5.1.1, General Requirements. Requires implementations to check that keys are correct for the chosen algorithm (P-256/P-384/P-521 curves paired with the matching ECDSA algorithms, edwards25519 for Ed25519, RSA moduli of at least 2048 bits, and ML-DSA parameter sets matching the identifier) and to refuse incorrect keys, while allowing refusal of RSA keys over 16384 bits. Recommends deterministic signature algorithms for live media streaming so segment caching is not harmed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s157-12-3-2-use-of-cose",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3.2. Use of COSE",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s157-12-3-2-use-of-cose.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 12.3.2. Use of COSE\n\nThe signature for the CBOR-encoded claim is produced by CBOR Object Signing and Encryption (COSE) as described in [RFC 9052](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9052), sections 4.2 and 4.4.\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> Payloads can either be present inside a COSE signature, or transported separately (\"detached content\" as described in [RFC 9052](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9052), section 4.1). In \"detached content\" mode, the signed data is stored externally to the `COSE_Sign1_Tagged` structure, and the `payload` field of the `COSE_Sign1_Tagged` structure is always `nil`.\n\nRegar",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Specifies that the CBOR-encoded claim is signed using COSE as described in RFC 9052, sections 4.2 and 4.4, noting that payloads may be embedded or detached, in which case the COSE_Sign1_Tagged payload field is nil. Requires that the payload field of the in-memory Sig_structure always be populated with the relevant external data and never be nil. For standard or update manifests, that payload is the contents of the claim JUMBF box, as described in Section 10.3.2.4, Signing a Claim, and Section 11.5, Serialization.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s158-12-3-3-computing-the-signature",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3.3. Computing the Signature",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s158-12-3-3-computing-the-signature.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 12.3.3. Computing the Signature\n\nThe signature is computed or verified as described in [RFC 9052](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9052), section 4.4. The following additional requirements apply to the construction of `Sig_structure`:\n\n*   The value for the `context` element shall be `Signature1` except where a particular use of digital signatures in this specification specifies using `CounterSignature` instead. `Signature` shall not be used.\n    \n*   The value for the `payload` element will be specified by each use of digital signatures in this specification.\n    \n*   The `external_aad`",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Specifies additional requirements for building Sig_structure under RFC 9052, section 4.4: the context shall be Signature1 (or CounterSignature where specified, never Signature), external_aad shall be a zero-length bstr, the payload is set by each use, and the alg header shall be in body_protected. Requires all C2PA digital signatures to be COSE_Sign1_Tagged structures with the same alg header in the protected bucket. Requires a detached payload to be nil and an embedded payload to be stored as a bstr, noting that a zero-length byte array cannot indicate detached content.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s159-12-3-4-adding-a-claimed-time-of-signing",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3.4. Adding a claimed time of signing",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s159-12-3-4-adding-a-claimed-time-of-signing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 12.3.4. Adding a claimed time of signing\n\nA claim generator may also wish to establish a \"claimed time of signing\" by adding an `iat` protected header, whose value is a `NumericDate`. If present, it shall represent the time at which the signature was generated.\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> A `NumericDate` is a CBOR numeric date (as described in [RFC 8949](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8949), section 3.4.2) but with the leading tag 1 (epoch-based date/time) omitted. It is not used anywhere else in this specification.\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> This recommendation is based on in-process updates to [JAdES](https:",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Permits a claim generator to add an iat protected header with a NumericDate value as a claimed time of signing, which, if present, shall represent when the signature was generated. Notes that NumericDate is a CBOR numeric date without the leading tag 1, and that the recommendation is based on in-process JAdES updates for a generator-provided timestamp useful when no trusted time source is available but not used for certificate validity checking.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s160-12-3-5-signature-validation",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "12.3.5. Signature Validation",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s160-12-3-5-signature-validation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 12.3.5. Signature Validation\n\nWhen producing a signature, if the claim generator can also act as a validator, the claim generator should validate that the signing credential is acceptable according to [Chapter 13, _Trust Model_](#_trust_model) and produce a warning if it is not. The claim generator may still allow signing with that credential if so desired. This may be desirable if it is known that the local claim generator’s validator has a different configuration than validators used by the expected audience of the asset.\n\n<a id=\"_cryptographic_validation\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends that a claim generator able to act as a validator check that its signing credential is acceptable under Chapter 13, Trust Model, and warn if it is not. Permits signing with that credential anyway, for instance when the local validator's configuration differs from that of validators used by the asset's expected audience.",
        "state": "draft",
