      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s179-13-5-1-1-general-requirements",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "13.5.1.1. General Requirements",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s179-13-5-1-1-general-requirements.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 13.5.1.1. General Requirements\n\nThis section defines the requirements to validate that an X.509 certificate is acceptable as a signing credential as described in [Section 14.7, “Validate the Signature”](#_validate_the_signature).\n\nAll C2PA Claim signing certificates shall fulfill the following requirements. The requirements do not apply to TSA, OCSP or CA certificates in certificate chains.\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> The `signatureAlgorithm` field identifies the algorithm the issuing Certificate Authority (CA) used to sign the certificate itself. This is distinct from the algorithm used for the C2PA",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines requirements for an X.509 certificate to be acceptable as a C2PA claim signing credential, not applying to TSA, OCSP or CA certificates. Requires the signatureAlgorithm to be one of the listed ECDSA, RSA, RSASSA-PSS, Ed25519 or ML-DSA identifiers, with constraints on RSASSA-PSS parameters, absent parameters for ML-DSA, named curves prime256v1, secp384r1 or secp521r1 for EC keys, and RSA moduli of at least 2048 bits; a note explains that RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 is permitted for CA certificate signatures but not for C2PA claims. For all certificates outside the private credential store, requires v3, no unique IDs, correct Basic Constraints (only end entity certificates sign claims, time stamps and OCSP responses), Authority and Subject Key Identifiers as prescribed, and a Key Usage extension with digitalSignature for manifest signers. Requires a non-empty EKU extension in end entity certificates, forbidding anyExtendedKeyUsage, requiring id-kp-timeStamping or id-kp-OCSPSigning for those purposes exclusively, and not rejecting certificates for unrelated extra EKUs.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s180-13-5-1-2-certificate-trust-chain",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "13.5.1.2. Certificate Trust Chain",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s180-13-5-1-2-certificate-trust-chain.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 13.5.1.2. Certificate Trust Chain\n\nWhen validating a certificate as the signing credential, if the certificate is present in the private credential store for X.509 certificates, the certificate is accepted. The private credential store is not consulted when validating time stamps.\n\nIf the certificate is not present in the private credential store, or the validator does not implement one, the trust chain shall be built and validated according to the procedure in [RFC 5280](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280), section 6 for the particular purpose required (signing, time stamping, or OC",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Specifies that a signing certificate in the private credential store is accepted, while otherwise the trust chain shall be built and validated per RFC 5280, section 6, for the relevant purpose and trust anchor store, with any failure causing rejection; the private credential store is never used to build chains or validate time stamps. Requires validators to reject certificates used for purposes not authorized by their EKU, and requires claim signing certificates to have at least one EKU for which the validator has trust anchors, using only those anchors. Requires validators to verify Certificate Profile compliance (outside the private credential store), including EKU presence and authorization for no more than one of C2PA signing, time stamp signing or OCSP signing, and to ignore any EKU present on CA certificates.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s181-13-5-2-certificate-revocation",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "13.5.2. Certificate Revocation",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s181-13-5-2-certificate-revocation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 13.5.2. Certificate Revocation\n\nX.509 certificates support revocation status queries. A claim generator should use the Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP, [RFC 6960](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6960)) and OCSP stapling (as originally conceptualized in [RFC 6066, Section 8](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6066), but implemented as described in this clause) to implement revocation. The claim generator shall not use Certificate Revocation Lists (CRLs, [RFC 5280](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280)).\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> Using CRLs requires downloading the entire list of revoked c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends that claim generators implement revocation using OCSP and OCSP stapling, and forbids them from using Certificate Revocation Lists, noting CRLs are slow to download and large. Recommends that CAs include an AuthorityInfoAccess extension, and requires that, when present, revocation information be stored in an unprotected COSE_Sign1 header labelled rVals following the given CDDL with an ocspVals array. Recommends that before signing, a claim generator query the OCSP service for the signer's certificate and any included intermediate CA certificates and store the responses in ocspVals, with validation described in Validate the Credential Revocation Information.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
