      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.3.6.1. Description",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s370-16-3-6-1-description.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 16.3.6.1. Description\n\nFiles that are written only by appending complete lines to the end of the file, such as application logs, event logs and JSON Lines streams, grow continuously and cannot be re-signed as a whole each time content is added. For these assets a single hard binding over the complete file would be invalidated by the next line written.\n\nA segment hash assertion binds a manifest to one bounded segment of such a file — the bytes added since the previous checkpoint — rather than to the file as a whole. Each segment hash assertion also records the position and length of the s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the problem of append-only files such as application logs, event logs and JSON Lines streams, where a single hard binding over the whole file would be invalidated by the next line written. Explains that a segment hash assertion binds a manifest to one bounded segment, the bytes added since the previous checkpoint, and records the position and length of the sentinel line carrying its manifest plus a digest of the preceding checkpoint's sentinel line so history cannot be rewritten without breaking the chain. Refers to Section A.3.17, Embedding manifests into append-only text files, for how checkpoints are written.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
