    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s458-16-6-2-2-1-description",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.6.2.2.1. Description",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s458-16-6-2-2-1-description.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.6.2.2.1. Description\n\nThis type is used to reference \"Original Preservation Image(OPI)\" in [Exif](https://www.cipa.jp/std/documents/download_e.html?CIPA_DC-008-2026-E). The OPI is introduced for recording authenticity of the image at the time of capture in which subsequent post image processing is not applied, along with metadata that describes image properties. This enables users to recognize the difference between the image at the time of capture and the main image developed through the image processing by camera systems when the main image has noticeable differences from the real",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes using the alternative content representation assertion to reference an Exif Original Preservation Image (OPI), the unprocessed capture-time image, either where it sits in the asset's MPF, located by its index in the parts array of the multi-asset hash assertion, or moved into the manifest, as typically happens when the format changes from Exif or the file becomes an ingredient. A moved OPI shall be recorded unchanged as an embedded data assertion, edits preserving the OPI should add a new assertion, removal shall be recorded with c2pa.deleted or by redaction rules, and a manifest shall not contain more than one such assertion labelled exif.originalPreservationImage.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s459-16-6-2-2-2-schema-and-example",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.6.2.2.2. Schema and Example",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s459-16-6-2-2-2-schema-and-example.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.6.2.2.2. Schema and Example\n\nThe schema for this assertion is defined by the `original-preservation-image-representation` rule in the following [CDDL Definition](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8610) (see [Section G.5.17, “Alternative Content Representation”](#cddl_annex_alt_content_rep) for the complete normative schema):\n\n```cddl\n; Describes a reference to an alternative version of the content\n; (e.g. an original preservation image in Exif, a secure capture from TEE, etc.)\nalternative-content-representation =\n  original-preservation-image-representation\n  / generic-representation",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The schema defines the c2pa.alternative-content-representation assertion in CDDL as either an original-preservation-image-representation (type exif.originalPreservationImage, with parameters multiAssetPartIndex or embeddedOriginalPreservationImage) or a generic-representation with a namespaced type and free-form parameters. It requires the type to be exif.originalPreservationImage and the parameters to reference the original preservation image either by its part index in an MPF asset or by a hashed URI to an embedded data assertion in the manifest, but not both, with CBOR examples of each case.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s460-16-6-3-asset-type",
