    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s517-16-9-1-10-2-localization-dictionary",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.1.10.2. Localization Dictionary",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s517-16-9-1-10-2-localization-dictionary.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.1.10.2. Localization Dictionary\n\nA localization dictionary shall consist of a single object, where each of its keys represents the translations using the language indexing technique from [JSON-LD](https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld/#language-indexing). If the value that requires translation is not associated with a top-level key, then \"dot notation\" (`.`) shall be used to reference keys nested in objects. Array indexing notation (`[n]`, `n>=0`) shall be used where a specific element in an array needs to be traversed. When the value requiring translation is itself an array, a specific el",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A localization dictionary shall be a single object whose keys map to translations indexed by language in the JSON-LD style, with dot notation used for nested keys and [n] array indexing for array elements, as shown in examples translating dc:title and actions[0].softwareAgent. Third-party keys and values must be namespaced (for example com.litware), and claim generators should supply their human-readable strings this way, as in an example localizing custom actions in a c2pa.actions assertion's metadata.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s518-16-9-2-regions-of-interest",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2. Regions of Interest",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s518-16-9-2-regions-of-interest.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### 16.9.2. Regions of Interest\n\n<a id=\"_description_29\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Heading that opens Section 16.9.2, Regions of Interest; the block holds no text beyond the heading.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s519-16-9-2-1-description",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.1. Description",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s519-16-9-2-1-description.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 16.9.2.1. Description\n\nIn some use cases, a given assertion, such as an [actions assertion](#_actions), may only be relevant to a specific portion of an asset as opposed to the entire asset. In those cases, it is necessary to have a way to describe that region - whether it be temporal, spatial, textual or a combination of them. A `region` definition serves that purpose.\n\n<a id=\"_common_2\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The description explains that some assertions, such as an actions assertion, apply only to part of an asset, and that a region definition is used to describe that temporal, spatial, textual or combined portion.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s520-16-9-2-2-common",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2. Common",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s520-16-9-2-2-common.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 16.9.2.2. Common\n\nThe most important part of the `region` definition is the `range` field which is used to describe a temporal range, a spatial range, a frame range, a textual range or a combination of them, for the region.\n\n> **NOTE:**\n> While the specification allows for specifying a combination of ranges, it is not defined how a Manifest Consumer will use them. It is expected that the C2PA’s User Experience Task Force will take this up in the future.\n\nA `region` may also contain one or more common fields:\n\n`name`\n\na free-text string representing a human-readable name for the region wh",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The central part of a region is its range field, which may describe temporal, spatial, frame or textual ranges or a combination, though a note says how consumers use combinations is not yet defined. A region may also carry the common fields name, identifier, type (from a controlled vocabulary such as IPTC's or an entity-specific value) and description, while the older role field is deprecated and shall no longer be generated.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s521-16-9-2-2-1-ranges",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.1. Ranges",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s521-16-9-2-2-1-ranges.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.1. Ranges\n\nAll ranges consist of a `type` field whose value is either \"spatial\", \"temporal\", \"frame\", \"textual\" or \"identified\". In addition, it shall contain one of the following fields whose data is an object consisting of the specific data for that range:\n\n*   `shape` (for spatial);\n    \n*   `time` (for temporal);\n    \n*   `frame` (for temporal or textual);\n    \n*   `text` (for textual);\n    \n*   `item` (for specifically identified items).\n    \n\n<a id=\"_spatial\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Every range has a type of spatial, temporal, frame, textual or identified, and shall contain the matching data object: shape, time, frame, text or item.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s522-16-9-2-2-2-spatial",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.2. Spatial",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s522-16-9-2-2-2-spatial.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.2. Spatial\n\nSpatial ranges are described using a `shape` object. A `shape` can be used to represent a rectangle, a circle or a polygon. It is modelled on the [Region Boundary Structure](http://www.iptc.org/std/photometadata/specification/IPTC-PhotoMetadata#region-boundary-structure) from the IPTC.\n\n<a id=\"_temporal\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Spatial ranges use a shape object that can represent a rectangle, circle or polygon, modelled on the IPTC Region Boundary Structure.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s523-16-9-2-2-3-temporal",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.3. Temporal",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s523-16-9-2-2-3-temporal.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.3. Temporal\n\nTemporal ranges are described using a `time` object, which represents a range from a starting time to an ending time. Times are normally described using a value that is relative to the start of the media. This is commonly referred to as \"Normal Play Time\" (`npt`) as described in Section 4.4.2 of [RFC 7826](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7826) (as recommended in [W3C Media Fragments specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/media-frags/#media-fragment-syntax)). Alternatively, it is possible to refer to an absolute time (e.g., \"10:00am\"). Absolute times, sometimes refe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Temporal ranges use a time object running from a start to an end, expressed either as Normal Play Time (npt) relative to the start of the media or as absolute wall-clock time in RFC 3339 format, with npt the default and missing start or end values meaning the beginning or end of the asset; starts are inclusive and ends exclusive unless endInclusivity is set to inclusive. For wallClock ranges the end shall not precede the start and at least one shall be given, with the missing bound derived from the asset duration or, if unknown as in live streams, left open, and implementations should use minimal decimal precision and snap to the nearest earlier sample or frame where alignment matters.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s524-16-9-2-2-4-frames",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.4. Frames",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s524-16-9-2-2-4-frames.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.4. Frames\n\nA `frame` object defines a range using the starting and ending frames or pages (inclusive). If no `start` is provided, the range shall start at the beginning of the asset. If no `end` is provided, the range shall end at the end of the asset. If neither is provided, the range shall represent the entire asset.\n\nFrames are represented as a single ordinal numbers, where `0` is the first frame.\n\nWhile frames are typically used to represent page numbers of a document, such as PDF, they may have uses in other media types, such as animation, video and audio. It is recommende",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A frame object defines an inclusive range of zero-based frames or pages, defaulting to the whole asset when start or end are absent, and while often used for document pages it may serve other media, though temporal ranges are recommended for time-based media where possible.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s525-16-9-2-2-5-textual",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.5. Textual",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s525-16-9-2-2-5-textual.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.5. Textual\n\nA `text` object defines a range using a one or more URL fragment identifiers, as defined by the [W3C Web Annotation fragment selector](https://www.w3.org/TR/annotation-model/#fragment-selector). It may also refine the range using offsets to the starting and ending characters (inclusive). If no `start` is provided, the range shall start at the beginning of the fragment. If no `end` is provided, the range shall end at the end of the fragment. If neither is provided, the range shall represent the entire fragment.\n\nWhen used singularly, the `fragment` entry of the `text",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A text object defines a range using one or more W3C Web Annotation fragment identifiers, optionally refined by inclusive start and end character offsets that default to the whole fragment. A text-selector-range-map pairs a selector with an optional end selector to form a contiguous range, and multiple pairs can describe a non-contiguous range.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s526-16-9-2-2-6-identified",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.2.6. Identified",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s526-16-9-2-2-6-identified.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "###### 16.9.2.2.6. Identified\n\nAn `item` object defines a media track, media item, or other discrete content item in the asset, allowing the claim generator to indicate assertions that apply to only a subset of the content carried in the asset’s file container. For example, it could be used to indicate that only the audio track of a video file is relevant.\n\nThe media or content item is identified by an `identifier` string whose value should match the typical item identification naming scheme in that specific container format. For example, the value of `identifier` should be `track_id` for MP4",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "An item object identifies a track, media item or other discrete content item in the asset's container, using an identifier that should follow the container's naming scheme (track_id for MP4, item_ID for HEIF) and a value, so that for example track 2 of an MP4 could be singled out. Identified ranges can also name a region by a semantic value from an ontology such as the Foundational Model of Anatomy, in which case the identifier shall be the URL or URI of that schema.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s527-16-9-2-3-schema",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.3. Schema",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s527-16-9-2-3-schema.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 16.9.2.3. Schema\n\nThe schema for this type is defined by the `region-map` rule in the following [CDDL Definition](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8610) (see [Section G.5.10, “Regions of Interest”](#cddl_annex_regions_of_interest) for the complete normative schema):\n\n```cddl\nregion-map = {\n    ; definition of the range, one or more ranges\n    \"region\":        [1* range-map],\n\n    ; a free-text string representing a human-readable name for the region which\n    ; could be used in a user interface.\n    ? \"name\": tstr .size (1..max-tstr-length),\n\n    ; a free-text string representing a mach",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The block gives the CDDL schema for the region-map rule, pointing to Section G.5.10 for the complete normative version. It defines region-map with a required array of range-map entries plus optional name, identifier, type, deprecated role, description and metadata, and range-map with a type choice of spatial, temporal, frame, textual or identified and the corresponding shape, time, frame, text or item objects. Supporting rules define shape-map (rectangle, circle or polygon in pixel or percent units with origin, size, inside and vertices), npt and wallClock time maps with regex-constrained start and end and endInclusivity, frame-map, the text selector maps, and item-map with identifier and value.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s528-16-9-2-4-examples",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "16.9.2.4. Examples",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s528-16-9-2-4-examples.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### 16.9.2.4. Examples\n\nA series of examples in CBOR diagnostic notation ([RFC 8949](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8949), clause 8) are shown below:\n\n```none\n// example of a combined temporal and spatial range in a video //\n{\n  \"region\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"temporal\",\n      \"time\": {\n        \"type\": \"npt\",\n        \"start\": \"0\",\n        \"end\": \"5.2\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"spatial\",\n      \"shape\": {\n        \"type\": \"rectangle\",\n        \"unit\": \"pixel\",\n        \"origin\": {\n          \"x\": 10.0,\n          \"y\": 10.0\n        },\n        \"width\": 200.0,\n        \"height\": 112.0",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The block gives a series of region examples in CBOR diagnostic notation. They cover a combined temporal and spatial range in a video, textual ranges in DOCX, tagged and untagged PDF, Excel cells and table cells, a frame range for deleted PDF pages, a specific video track via an identified range, and an anatomy-based identified range spanning the whole asset.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s529-17-patent-policy",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "17\\. Patent Policy",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s529-17-patent-policy.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## 17\\. Patent Policy\n\nThe C2PA has adopted an open standard patent policy via W3C’s Patent Mode (2004):\n\n**Licensing Commitment**. For materials other than source code or datasets developed by the Working Group, each Working Group Participant agrees to make available any of its Essential Claims, as defined in the W3C Patent Policy (available at [http://www.w3.org/Consortium/Patent-Policy-20040205](http://www.w3.org/Consortium/Patent-Policy-20040205)), under the W3C RF licensing requirements Section 5 ([http://www.w3.org/Consortium/Patent-Policy-20040205](http://www.w3.org/Consortium/Patent-Po",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The patent policy states that the C2PA follows the W3C Patent Policy (2004), with each Working Group Participant committing to license its Essential Claims in Approved Deliverables on W3C royalty-free terms, while source code is covered by the Working Group charter licence. Before a draft is approved a participant may exclude Essential Claims by written Exclusion Notice to the chair, identifying the patents or applications concerned, with any claims not listed remaining under the commitment and the chair publishing the notices.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s530-appendix-a-embedding-manifests",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Appendix A: Embedding manifests",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s530-appendix-a-embedding-manifests.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## Appendix A: Embedding manifests\n\n<a id=\"_supported_formats\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Heading that opens Appendix A, Embedding manifests; the block holds no text beyond the heading.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
