    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s668-appendix-b-user-experience",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Appendix B: User Experience",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s668-appendix-b-user-experience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "## Appendix B: User Experience\n\nThis annex is a non-normative summary of the C2PA approach to user experience (UX) for provenance. The definitive, actively evolving guidance for implementers is the [User Experience Recommendations](../../2.2/ux/UX_Recommendations.html.md) document.\n\n<a id=\"_principles\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Appendix B is a non-normative summary of the C2PA approach to user experience for provenance, deferring to the separate User Experience Recommendations document as the definitive guidance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s669-b-1-principles",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.1. Principles",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s669-b-1-principles.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### B.1. Principles\n\nUX designed for the consumption of C2PA provenance shall be informed by the context of the asset. The recommendations are built around four primary user groups — Consumers, Creators, Publishers and Verifiers (or Investigators), as defined in the [C2PA Guiding Principles](https://c2pa.org/principles/) — and describe exemplary interfaces for each across common contexts. These are recommendations, not mandates, and are expected to evolve; see the UX Recommendations document for full detail.\n\n<a id=\"_disclosure_levels\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Provenance UX must be informed by the asset's context, and the recommendations, which are not mandates, describe interfaces for consumers, creators, publishers and verifiers across common contexts.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s670-b-2-disclosure-levels",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.2. Disclosure Levels",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s670-b-2-disclosure-levels.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### B.2. Disclosure Levels\n\nBecause the complete set of C2PA data for a given asset can be overwhelming to a user, the UX Recommendations document defines four levels of progressive disclosure, summarized below:\n\n*   Level 1: An indication that C2PA data is present and its cryptographic validation status.\n    \n*   Level 2: A summary of C2PA data available for a given asset. This level should provide enough information for the particular content, user, and context to allow the consumer to understand to a sufficient degree how the asset came to its current state.\n    \n*   Level 3: A detailed dis",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists four progressive disclosure levels for C2PA data, from an indication of presence and validation status, through a summary and a detailed provenance view, to a forensic tool showing signatures and trust signals; the block ends with a stray \"== Information security\" heading fragment, apparently from extraction.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s671-b-3-threats-and-security-considerations",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3. Threats and Security Considerations",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s671-b-3-threats-and-security-considerations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "### B.3. Threats and Security Considerations\n\nInformation security and privacy are principal concerns of C2PA. This section is a non-normative summary; the threat modelling process, the resulting threat model, security considerations for implementers, and related best practices are maintained separately at [Security Considerations](../../2.4/security/Security_Considerations.html.md).\n\n<a id=\"_resource_exhaustion_when_hashing_compressed_members\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the threats and security considerations section, stating that information security and privacy are principal concerns of C2PA and that this section is a non-normative summary, with the threat model, implementer considerations and best practices maintained in a separate Security Considerations document.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s672-b-3-1-resource-exhaustion-when-hashing-compresse",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3.1. Resource exhaustion when hashing compressed members",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s672-b-3-1-resource-exhaustion-when-hashing-compresse.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### B.3.1. Resource exhaustion when hashing compressed members\n\nWhere a hard binding is computed over the uncompressed bytes of a compressed asset member, a validator shall decompress attacker-supplied data before it can decide whether the asset is trustworthy. A member stored with a high compression ratio can expand to a size far larger than its stored form, so a small input can be made to consume unbounded memory or time during validation. This applies to any format whose members are hashed after decompression, including the per-record compression commonly used with WARC files (see [Section",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that when a hard binding is computed over the uncompressed bytes of a compressed member, a highly compressed member can make validation consume unbounded memory or time, for example with per-record compressed WARC files. Before evaluating the signature, a validator shall enforce an implementation-defined maximum decompressed member size and shall reject any member whose decompressed length disagrees with the length declared in its own metadata, checks a note says exist for adversarial input so that hashing uncompressed bytes stays safe.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s673-b-3-2-unicode-security-in-text-fields",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3.2. Unicode security in text fields",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s673-b-3-2-unicode-security-in-text-fields.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "#### B.3.2. Unicode security in text fields\n\nC2PA Manifests contain text string fields that are displayed to users or compared programmatically, including `dc:title`, `description`, claim generator identifiers, and action `reason` values. These fields are susceptible to Unicode-based attacks that can mislead users or undermine provenance integrity.\n\n<a id=\"_bidirectional_overrides\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that manifest text fields shown to users or compared programmatically, such as dc:title, description, claim generator identifiers and action reason values, are open to Unicode-based attacks that can mislead users or undermine provenance integrity.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s674-b-3-2-1-bidirectional-overrides",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3.2.1. Bidirectional overrides",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s674-b-3-2-1-bidirectional-overrides.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### B.3.2.1. Bidirectional overrides\n\nBidirectional override characters (U+202A through U+202E) can reorder displayed text to misrepresent field content, as demonstrated by the Trojan Source attack ([CVE-2021-42574](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2021-42574)). Claim generators shall not include bidirectional override characters in text fields. Bidirectional isolate characters (U+2066 through U+2069) are permitted in fields containing mixed-direction text.\n\n<a id=\"_confusable_characters\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Claim generators shall not include bidirectional override characters (U+202A to U+202E) in text fields, since they can reorder displayed text as in the Trojan Source attack, though bidirectional isolate characters (U+2066 to U+2069) are permitted in mixed-direction text.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s675-b-3-2-2-confusable-characters",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3.2.2. Confusable characters",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s675-b-3-2-2-confusable-characters.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### B.3.2.2. Confusable characters\n\nConfusable characters from different scripts can impersonate legitimate identifiers. For example, Cyrillic \"а\" (U+0430) is visually indistinguishable from Latin \"a\" (U+0061) in most fonts. Manifest consumers that display identity-bearing fields (claim generator name, `dc:title`, signer identity) should apply confusable detection as defined in [Unicode Technical Standard #39](https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr39/) before presenting these values to users.\n\n<a id=\"_normalization_2\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Manifest consumers that display identity-bearing fields such as the claim generator name, dc:title or signer identity should apply confusable detection per Unicode Technical Standard #39, because look-alike characters from other scripts can impersonate legitimate identifiers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "claude-opus-5-5"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-01"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/c2pa-2026-specification-2-5/blocks/prose-s676-b-3-2-3-normalization",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B.3.2.3. Normalization",
      "pageStart": null,
      "pageEnd": null,
      "target": "../sections/s676-b-3-2-3-normalization.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "##### B.3.2.3. Normalization\n\nText fields that are not in a consistent Unicode normalization form can produce different hash values for semantically identical content, affecting search, comparison, and deduplication. Claim generators should produce text string fields in Unicode Normalization Form C (NFC), consistent with [Character Model for the World Wide Web: String Matching](https://www.w3.org/TR/charmod-norm/).\n\n<a id=\"_adversarial_control_characters\"></a>",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Claim generators should produce text string fields in Unicode Normalization Form C (NFC), since inconsistent normalization can give semantically identical text different hash values and disrupt search, comparison and deduplication.",
        "state": "draft",
